<DocsLayout title="What is Identity">
  <h1>What is Identity</h1>
  <p>
    Identity is a single-container authentication and user-management service
    backed by a relational datastore (Postgres for production, embedded SQLite
    for a single node, in-memory for tests).
    It is the auth half of the
    <em>"two-container product"</em> shape: one container owns user data and
    issues JWTs; one container per product owns the product data.
  </p>

  <h2>Design goals</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>One image, deployed once per product.</strong> Each deployment
      serves a Project (<code>GATEWAY_DEFAULT_PROJECT_ID</code>), and the
      Project is the isolation boundary — its own user pool. Two products
      sharing one Postgres still get isolation because every query is scoped
      by <code>WHERE project_id = $1</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stateless service container.</strong> All durable state is in
      the datastore (Postgres or SQLite). The Identity container can be
      replaced or scaled horizontally without data migration.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Standards-shaped tokens.</strong> Access tokens are RS256 JWTs
      with a <code>kid</code> header; downstream services verify them against
      <code>/.well-known/jwks.json</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Defence-in-depth.</strong> bcrypt cost 12 for passwords, AES-GCM
      encryption for TOTP secrets at rest, refresh-token rotation, audit
      logging on every security-relevant action, failed-login lockout.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What it is not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Not an authorization server.</strong> Identity issues JWTs with
      a coarse <code>role</code> claim (<code>admin</code> /
      <code>member</code> / <code>guest</code>); fine-grained access control
      is the consuming service's responsibility.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Not an OAuth IdP.</strong> Identity <em>consumes</em> OAuth
      tokens from upstream providers (Google, Microsoft); it does not act as
      an OAuth provider for third parties.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>One Project per deployment.</strong> A deployment serves one
      default Project, and the Project is the isolation shard. Tenants form
      automatically underneath it, one per verified email domain — they are
      derived from the authenticated user, never a login input.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Surface area</h2>
  <p>
    A single Connect-RPC service (<code>identity.v1.IdentityService</code>) over
    HTTP/JSON. Browser clients can call it directly with
    <a href="https://connectrpc.com/docs/web/">Connect-Web</a>; Go services
    use <a href="https://connectrpc.com/docs/go/">Connect-Go</a>. There is no
    bespoke REST gateway — Connect handles JSON serialization.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
